<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tumen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A tumen was a military unit of ten thousand soldiers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a> and other steppe armies. It formed the largest division in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/decimal-system">decimal system</a> that organised the army into units of ten, one hundred, one thousand and ten thousand. Accounts of the Mongol Empire treat this system as central to Mongol military effectiveness, since it broke up old tribal loyalties and tied soldiers to commanders appointed by the khan.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…in Sejong's reign, the retired king Taejong sent an expedition against Tsushima to suppress Japanese pirates, and campaigns against the Jurchen in the 1430s and 1440s established the four commanderies and six garrisons that fixed the northern border on the Yalu and <b>Tumen</b> rivers, where it still lies.</blockquote><a href="/empire/joseon">Joseon · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Armies were divided into decimal units (arban, zuun, mingghan, <b>tumen</b>) for command and control, ensuring flexibility and scalability.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its location is uncertain, perhaps in the <b>Tumen</b> river region.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-buyeo">Eastern Buyeo</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1215 he rebelled himself and proclaimed the kingdom of Great Zhen, later called Eastern Xia, moving north-east into the old Jurchen homeland along the <b>Tumen</b> and Ussuri rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/eastern-xia">Eastern Xia</a></li><li><blockquote>The khans ruled a shifting federation of Mongol and Oirat <b>tumens</b> rather than a centralised state.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-yuan">Northern Yuan</a></li><li><blockquote>They were a nomadic warrior confederation of mixed Mongol and Turkic origin, organised in <b>tumens</b> under their own commanders.</blockquote><a href="/state/qaraunas">Qara'unas</a></li></ul></section>
